__d(function(e,t,i,n,o,a,r){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Class 7 (NCERT)",chapter_number:9,chapter_title:"Rational Numbers",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Define a rational number. What are the conditions?",back:"A rational number is any number that can be written as p/q where: (1) p and q are both integers, and (2) q \u2260 0 (denominator is never zero). Examples: 3/7, \u22124/5, 0/1 (= 0), 6/1 (= 6), \u22123/\u22127 (= 3/7). All fractions, all integers, and all negative fractions are rational numbers.",memory_hook:"RATIONAL = RATIO of integers. p/q is a RATIO of two integers. Remember: q can NEVER be 0 \u2014 dividing by zero is the one rule you can NEVER break in maths!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is every integer a rational number? Give an example.",back:"Yes \u2014 every integer n is a rational number because it can be written as n/1. Examples: 5 = 5/1, \u22128 = \u22128/1, 0 = 0/1. So the set of rational numbers includes all integers, all positive fractions, and all negative fractions. Rational numbers are an extension of the number system beyond just integers.",memory_hook:"INTEGER \u2192 RATIONAL is easy: just put /1 under it! 7 \u2192 7/1. \u22123 \u2192 \u22123/1. 0 \u2192 0/1. Every integer is a rational number in disguise \u2014 wearing a '/1' costume!"},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the standard form of a rational number? What are its two conditions?",back:"A rational number p/q is in standard form when: (1) The denominator q is positive (q > 0). (2) The HCF of |p| (absolute value of numerator) and q = 1 \u2014 meaning numerator and denominator share no common factor other than 1. To reduce to standard form: step 1 \u2014 make denominator positive; step 2 \u2014 divide both by HCF. Example: \u22128/12 \u2192 HCF(8,12)=4 \u2192 \u22122/3 (standard form).",memory_hook:"Standard Form = POSITIVE denominator + FULLY REDUCED. Think of it as tidying up: the denominator must be 'upright' (positive) and the fraction must be 'trimmed' (no common factors). A neat, tidy rational number!"},{id:"fc04",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Where do positive and negative rational numbers lie on the number line?",back:"On the number line: positive rational numbers lie to the RIGHT of zero; negative rational numbers lie to the LEFT of zero. Zero (0) is neither positive nor negative \u2014 it sits at the centre. The further right, the greater the value. The further left, the smaller (more negative) the value. All positive rationals > 0 > all negative rationals.",memory_hook:"NUMBER LINE: RIGHT = POSITIVE (richer!), LEFT = NEGATIVE (poorer!). Think of it as a financial scale \u2014 positive money is to the right, debt is to the left, and 0 is breaking even!"},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many rational numbers exist between any two rational numbers?",back:"Infinitely many rational numbers exist between any two distinct rational numbers. For example, between 1/3 and 1/2, there are infinitely many rational numbers including 5/12, 7/18, 2/5, etc. This property \u2014 that rational numbers are 'dense' (infinitely many between any two) \u2014 distinguishes them from integers, where there may be no integer between two consecutive integers.",memory_hook:"INFINITE rationals between any two = the number line is INFINITELY CROWDED between any two points. It's like zooming into a Google Map \u2014 no matter how close two points seem, you can always zoom in more and find more points between them!"},{id:"fc06",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the additive inverse of a rational number? Give an example.",back:"The additive inverse of a rational number p/q is \u2212p/q. When you add a number to its additive inverse, the result is 0. Example: additive inverse of 3/7 is \u22123/7, because 3/7 + (\u22123/7) = 0. Additive inverse of \u22125/8 is 5/8. The additive inverse is also called the 'opposite' of the number. Every rational number has exactly one additive inverse.",memory_hook:"ADDITIVE INVERSE = the opposite that cancels you out to ZERO. It's your maths 'nemesis' \u2014 you two together equal nothing! 3/7 + its nemesis (\u22123/7) = 0. Perfect cancellation!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the reciprocal (multiplicative inverse) of a rational number? Give an example.",back:"The reciprocal of p/q is q/p \u2014 flip the numerator and denominator. When a number is multiplied by its reciprocal, the result is 1. Example: reciprocal of 3/7 is 7/3, because (3/7) \xd7 (7/3) = 21/21 = 1. Reciprocal of \u22122/5 is \u22125/2. Reciprocal of 4 (=4/1) is 1/4. Zero (0/1) has NO reciprocal because 1/0 is undefined.",memory_hook:"RECIPROCAL = FLIP IT! p/q \u2192 q/p. Think of flipping a pancake \u2014 what was on top goes to bottom and vice versa. And multiplied together they give 1 \u2014 a perfect pair!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the rule for adding/subtracting rational numbers with different denominators?",back:"To add or subtract rational numbers with different denominators: Step 1 \u2014 Find the LCM of the denominators. Step 2 \u2014 Convert each fraction to an equivalent fraction with the LCM as denominator. Step 3 \u2014 Add or subtract the numerators (keep denominator as LCM). Step 4 \u2014 Reduce to standard form. Example: 1/4 + 1/6: LCM=12 \u2192 3/12 + 2/12 = 5/12.",memory_hook:"ADD/SUB rational numbers: LCM \u2192 Convert \u2192 Operate \u2192 Reduce. Remember: LCOR! You cannot add fractions with different denominators any more than you can add apples and oranges \u2014 you need a COMMON unit (denominator) first!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the sign rule for multiplication of rational numbers.",back:"Sign rules for multiplication: (positive) \xd7 (positive) = positive. (negative) \xd7 (negative) = positive. (positive) \xd7 (negative) = negative. (negative) \xd7 (positive) = negative. Summary: SAME signs \u2192 POSITIVE result. DIFFERENT signs \u2192 NEGATIVE result. Example: (\u22123/4) \xd7 (\u22122/5) = +6/20 = 3/10 (both negative \u2192 positive). (3/4) \xd7 (\u22122/5) = \u22126/20 = \u22123/10 (different signs \u2192 negative).",memory_hook:"SIGNS: Same \u2192 Smile (positive +). Different \u2192 Disagree (negative \u2212). Two negatives = two people disagreeing with each other's disagreement = they AGREE = positive! Mathematical peacemaking!"},{id:"fc10",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you divide one rational number by another?",back:"To divide p/q by r/s: multiply p/q by the RECIPROCAL of r/s. Formula: (p/q) \xf7 (r/s) = (p/q) \xd7 (s/r) = ps/qr. Remember the KCF rule: KEEP the first fraction, CHANGE \xf7 to \xd7, FLIP the second fraction. Example: (3/5) \xf7 (2/7) = (3/5) \xd7 (7/2) = 21/10. Apply sign rules for the final answer.",memory_hook:"KCF = Keep, Change, Flip. (3/5) \xf7 (2/7): KEEP 3/5, CHANGE \xf7 to \xd7, FLIP 2/7 to 7/2 \u2192 (3/5) \xd7 (7/2) = 21/10. KCF is your division superpower \u2014 never forget it!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"How do you compare two rational numbers with different denominators?",back:"To compare rational numbers p/q and r/s: Step 1 \u2014 Find LCM of q and s. Step 2 \u2014 Convert both to equivalent fractions with LCM denominator: p/q = (p \xd7 LCM/q) / LCM and r/s = (r \xd7 LCM/s) / LCM. Step 3 \u2014 Compare the new numerators \u2014 larger numerator = larger rational number. Example: Compare \u22123/4 and \u22125/6. LCM=12. \u22123/4 = \u22129/12, \u22125/6 = \u221210/12. Since \u22129 > \u221210, we have \u22123/4 > \u22125/6.",memory_hook:"Compare rationals: LCM \u2192 Same denominator \u2192 Compare numerators. Think of making all fractions 'speak the same language' (same denominator) before comparing. You can't compare apples and oranges \u2014 convert to grams first!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"How do you find rational numbers between two given rational numbers?",back:"Method: Step 1 \u2014 Find LCM of denominators and convert. If not enough numbers between numerators, multiply both fractions by a suitable number. Step 2 \u2014 List fractions with numerators between the two converted numerators. Example: Between 1/3 and 1/2. LCM=6: 2/6 and 3/6. Only one integer between 2 and 3. Multiply both by 5: 10/30 and 15/30. Rational numbers: 11/30, 12/30 (=2/5), 13/30, 14/30 (=7/15).",memory_hook:"Finding rationals between two: ZOOM IN by multiplying both by a bigger number. Like zooming into a map \u2014 suddenly many more locations appear between two points that seemed close!"},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why is 0 a special rational number? How does it behave in operations?",back:"0 is special because: (1) 0 is a rational number (0 = 0/1). (2) 0 is neither positive nor negative. (3) 0 is the ADDITIVE IDENTITY: any rational number + 0 = that number. (4) 0 \xd7 (any rational) = 0. (5) 0 \xf7 (any non-zero rational) = 0. (6) You CANNOT divide by 0 \u2014 it is undefined. (7) 0 has no multiplicative inverse (reciprocal) because 1/0 is undefined.",memory_hook:"ZERO: Identity for addition (adds nothing), Destroyer for multiplication (everything becomes 0), FORBIDDEN as a denominator or divisor. Zero is the wildcard of rational numbers \u2014 powerful in some ways, impossible in others!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Reduce \u221215/35 to standard form. Show all steps.",back:"Step 1: Check the denominator \u2014 35 is positive \u2713. Step 2: Find HCF(15, 35). Factors of 15: 1,3,5,15. Factors of 35: 1,5,7,35. HCF = 5. Step 3: Divide both by 5: \u221215\xf75 / 35\xf75 = \u22123/7. Step 4: Check \u2014 denominator 7 is positive \u2713; HCF(3,7) = 1 \u2713. Answer: Standard form of \u221215/35 = \u22123/7.",memory_hook:"\u221215/35 \u2192 HCF is 5 \u2192 divide both \u2192 \u22123/7. Steps: positive denominator? \u2713 \u2192 find HCF \u2192 divide both \u2192 done. Two-step tidy-up for every standard form problem!"},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Calculate: (\u22122/3) + (4/5). Show steps.",back:"Step 1: Different denominators \u2192 find LCM(3,5) = 15. Step 2: Convert \u22122/3: multiply by 5/5 \u2192 \u221210/15. Step 3: Convert 4/5: multiply by 3/3 \u2192 12/15. Step 4: Add: (\u221210 + 12)/15 = 2/15. Step 5: Check standard form: denominator 15 > 0 \u2713; HCF(2,15) = 1 \u2713. Answer: (\u22122/3) + (4/5) = 2/15.",memory_hook:"LCM of 3 and 5 = 15. \u22122/3 = \u221210/15, 4/5 = 12/15. \u221210 + 12 = 2. Answer: 2/15. LCOR in action: LCM(15) \u2192 Convert \u2192 Operate(\u221210+12=2) \u2192 Result(2/15)!"},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Calculate: (\u22123/7) \xd7 (14/9). Show steps.",back:"Step 1: Multiply numerators: (\u22123) \xd7 14 = \u221242. Step 2: Multiply denominators: 7 \xd7 9 = 63. Step 3: Result: \u221242/63. Step 4: Reduce \u2014 HCF(42,63) = 21. Divide both by 21: \u22122/3. Step 5: Signs \u2014 one negative, one positive \u2192 negative \u2713. Answer: (\u22123/7) \xd7 (14/9) = \u22122/3.",memory_hook:"PRO TIP: Cancel BEFORE multiplying to save work! \u22123/7 \xd7 14/9: 3 and 9 share factor 3 (\u2192 1 and 3); 14 and 7 share factor 7 (\u2192 2 and 1). So: (\u22121/1) \xd7 (2/3) = \u22122/3. Cross-cancellation = maths efficiency!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Calculate: (\u22124/5) \xf7 (8/15). Show full steps.",back:"Step 1: Apply KCF \u2014 Keep (\u22124/5), Change (\xf7 to \xd7), Flip (8/15 to 15/8). \u2192 (\u22124/5) \xd7 (15/8). Step 2: Multiply numerators: (\u22124) \xd7 15 = \u221260. Step 3: Multiply denominators: 5 \xd7 8 = 40. Step 4: Result: \u221260/40. Step 5: HCF(60,40) = 20. Divide both: \u22123/2. Answer: (\u22124/5) \xf7 (8/15) = \u22123/2.",memory_hook:"KCF: Keep \u22124/5, Change \xf7 to \xd7, Flip 8/15 to 15/8. Then: \u22124\xd715 = \u221260, 5\xd78 = 40, \u221260/40 = \u22123/2. Or cancel first: 4 and 8 \u2192 1 and 2; 15 and 5 \u2192 3 and 1. Answer: \u22123/2. Same result, less work!"},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Compare \u22125/6 and \u22127/9. Which is greater?",back:"Step 1: Find LCM(6,9) = 18. Step 2: Convert \u22125/6: multiply by 3/3 \u2192 \u221215/18. Step 3: Convert \u22127/9: multiply by 2/2 \u2192 \u221214/18. Step 4: Compare numerators: \u221215 vs \u221214. Since \u221214 > \u221215 (less negative), we have \u221214/18 > \u221215/18. Step 5: Therefore \u22127/9 > \u22125/6. Answer: \u22127/9 is greater than \u22125/6.",memory_hook:"Comparing negatives TRIPS students! \u221214 > \u221215 even though 14 < 15. On the number line: \u221214/18 is closer to 0 than \u221215/18, so it is GREATER. With negatives: LESS negative = GREATER value. Think of debt: owing \u20b914 is better than owing \u20b915!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Find two rational numbers between \u22121/2 and 0.",back:"Step 1: Convert to same denominator. \u22121/2 = \u22121/2 and 0 = 0/2. We need numbers between \u22121/2 and 0/2, but \u22121 to 0 has nothing in between as integers. Step 2: Multiply both by 4: \u22121/2 = \u22124/8 and 0 = 0/8. Step 3: Numbers between \u22124/8 and 0/8: \u22123/8, \u22122/8 (= \u22121/4), \u22121/8. Step 4: Two rational numbers between \u22121/2 and 0 are \u22123/8 and \u22121/4.",memory_hook:"Between \u22121/2 and 0? ZOOM IN by multiplying by 4 \u2192 \u22124/8 to 0/8 \u2192 pick \u22123/8 and \u22122/8. Zooming into the number line between negatives works just like between positives!"},{id:"fc20",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Why does the definition of rational numbers require q \u2260 0? What goes wrong if q = 0?",back:"If q = 0, then p/q means p \xf7 0 \u2014 dividing by zero. Division is defined as finding how many times the divisor fits into the dividend. No number (or infinite numbers) can be multiplied by 0 to give p (if p \u2260 0), so p/0 has no defined value. If p = 0, then 0/0 is indeterminate (any number \xd7 0 = 0, so no unique answer). Division by zero creates logical contradictions in mathematics. Therefore q = 0 is strictly forbidden.",memory_hook:"q \u2260 0 = the GOLDEN RULE of rational numbers. Breaking it = mathematical chaos. It's like dividing a pizza among 0 people \u2014 how much does each person get? The question makes no sense! Zero denominator = mathematical nonsense."}]}},2962,[]);